ting fd 1 to capture what the C `driver` prints is process-global, and\n# libtest writes its own progress to fd 1 from other threads.  This test target\n# therefore brings its own single-threaded `main` instead of libtest's harness.\n[[test]]\nname = \"in_process_stdout\"\npath = \"tests/in_process_stdout.rs\"\nharness = false\n\n[profile.release]\npanic = \"abort\"\n\n[workspace]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":31,"oldLines":4,"newStart":31,"newLines":16,"lines":[" [profile.release]"," panic = \"abort\""," ","+# The C functions take bare `int *` and dereference them with no NULL check, so","+# an invalid pointer is a hardware fault (SIGSEGV) -- and a misaligned `int *`","+# is simply an unaligned load on x86-64.  rustc's `-C debug-assertions` inserts","+# null/alignment checks in front of every raw-pointer dereference, which turns","+# those faults into a Rust panic -> `abort()` (SIGABRT) inside an `extern \"C\"`","+# function.  That is a *behavioural* difference from the C, and it exists only","+# in the dev profile (release has the checks off), so switch it off here too:","+# the debug artifact must fault exactly like the release artifact and like C.","+[profile.dev]","+debug-assertions = false","+overflow-checks = false","+"," [workspace]"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
