{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01PRBsuMvWrZUD3DG2VLuf6L","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[package]\n2\tname = \"driver\"\n3\tversion = \"0.1.0\"\n4\tedition = \"2021\"\n5\t\n6\t# Shared object whose exported C-ABI symbols mirror the C build exactly\n7\t# (fma_array, driver, main).  `cdylib` only: no rlib is produced so that the\n8\t# integration tests cannot link the crate directly -- they must `dlopen` the\n9\t# resulting `libdriver.so`, exactly like an external consumer.\n10\t[lib]\n11\tname = \"driver\"\n12\tpath = \"src/lib.rs\"\n13\tcrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n14\t\n15\t[[bin]]\n16\tname = \"driver\"\n17\tpath = \"src/main.rs\"\n18\t\n19\t[dev-dependencies]\n20\tlibloading = \"0.8\"\n21\tlibc = \"0.2\"\n22\t\n23\t# Redirecting fd 1 to capture what the C `driver` prints is process-global, and\n24\t# libtest writes its own progress to fd 1 from other threads.  This test target\n25\t# therefore brings its own single-threaded `main` instead of libtest's harness.\n26\t[[test]]\n27\tname = \"in_process_stdout\"\n28\tpath = \"tests/in_process_stdout.rs\"\n29\tharness = false\n30\t\n31\t[profile.release]\n32\tpanic = \"abort\"\n33\t\n34\t# The C functions take bare `int *` and dereference them with no NULL check, so\n35\t# an invalid pointer is a hardware fault (SIGSEGV) -- and a misaligned `int *`\n36\t# is simply an unaligned load on x86-64.  rustc's `-C debug-assertions` inserts\n37\t# null/alignment checks in front of every raw-pointer dereference, which turns\n38\t# those faults into a Rust panic -> `abort()` (SIGABRT) inside an `extern \"C\"`\n39\t# function.  That is a *behavioural* difference from the C, and it exists only\n40\t# in the dev profile (release has the checks off), so switch it off here too:\n41\t# the debug artifact must fault exactly like the release artifact and like C.\n42\t[profile.dev]\n43\tdebug-assertions = false\n44\toverflow-checks = false\n45\t\n46\t[workspace]\n47\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"54b371d1-f01d-4012-be17-3d40a3cd679b","uuid":"6f3776b3-e81d-4c69-ba47-46a51aab03d6","timestamp":"2026-08-18T08:01:41.480Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-4T6V0s/translated_rust/Cargo.toml","content":"[package]\nname = \"driver\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\n\n# Shared object whose exported C-ABI symbols mirror the C build exactly\n# (fma_array, driver, main).  `cdylib` only: no rlib is produced so that the\n# integration tests cannot link the crate directly -- they must `dlopen` the\n# resulting `libdriver.so`, exactly like an external consumer.\n[lib]\nname = \"driver\"\npath = \"src/lib.rs\"\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\"]\n\n[[bin]]\nname = \"driver\"\npath = \"src/main.rs\"\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\nlibc = \"0.2\"\n\n# Redirecting fd 1 to capture what the C `driver` prints is process-global, and\n# libtest writes its own progress to fd 1 from other threads.  This test target\n# therefore brings its own single-threaded `main` instead of libtest's harness.\n[[test]]\nname = \"in_process_stdout\"\npath = \"tests/in_process_stdout.rs\"\nharness = false\n\n[profile.release]\npanic = \"abort\"\n\n# The C functions take bare `int *` and dereference them with no NULL check, so\n# an invalid pointer is a hardware fault (SIGSEGV) -- and a misaligned `int *`\n# is simply an unaligned load on x86-64.  rustc's `-C debug-assertions` inserts\n# null/alignment checks in front of every raw-pointer dereference, which turns\n# those faults into a Rust panic -> `abort()` (SIGABRT) inside an `extern \"C\"`\n# function.  That is a *behavioural* difference from the C, and it exists only\n# in the dev profile (release has the checks off), so switch it off here too:\n# the debug artifact must fault exactly like the release artifact and like C.\n[profile.dev]\ndebug-assertions = false\noverflow-checks = false\n\n[workspace]\n","numLines":47,"startLine":1,"totalLines":47}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_oqffccl2cx36kqf4r5gmf7jwagd66ezw7hlkhuog3onelistyp4a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. Here's what happened.\n\n## The project\n\n`c_src/src/main.c` is a single 49-line translation unit with three functions — `fma_array`, `driver`, `main` — and `CMakeLists.txt` builds it as an *executable*, not a library. So I built it both ways: the CMake program plus a shared object from the same TU with the same flags (`gcc -fPIC -shared`; CMake uses no `-O`/`-D`).\n\nOn the Rust side I split `src/main.rs` into `src/imp.rs` (logic), `src/lib.rs` (a `cdylib` with `#[no_mangle]` wrappers for all three C symbols) and `src/main.rs` (the program). The lib is **`cdylib`-only**, so the integration tests physically cannot link it — they must `dlopen` both `.so`s, which is what exercises the export wrappers.\n\n## Phase A artifacts\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C `.so` exports exactly `fma_array`, `driver`, `main`; the Rust `.so` exports the same three; all 50 of its undefined symbols are `@GLIBC`/`@GCC`. Diff is empty; nothing stubbed.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 24 rows. The C has no assert/enum/`return -1`/NULL check, so its whole rejection surface is `scanf(...) != 1` (each glibc failure mode), the `i < 100` capacity bound, the two `i < len` guards, and `(int) strtol(...)` clamping.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 40 rows over the real axes: entry point × `len` class × the 8 aliasing patterns of the four pointers × value class, plus token count/separator/spelling/magnitude classes for stdin. No `[features]` in Cargo.toml and no `option()`/`#ifdef` in the C ⇒ exactly one build configuration (enumerated by the script anyway).\n\n## Bugs found and fixed in the Rust\n\n1. **`fma_array` didn't exist as a general function.** The translation had only `fma_array_aliased` (the `driver` special case), so the exported 4-pointer entry point was missing. Rewrote it over raw pointers so any aliasing (partial forward/backward overlap included) loads all three operands before storing, exactly like the C.\n2. **NULL/misaligned pointers aborted instead of faulting.** C dies with `SIGSEGV`; the Rust dev build panicked (`null pointer dereference occurred`) → `abort()` → `SIGABRT`. Caused by rustc's `-C debug-assertions` UB checks, absent from release. Fixed with `[profile.dev] debug-assertions = false, overflow-checks = false` so debug faults like release and like C.\n3. **`stdin` wasn't persistent.** C's `stdin` is a global `FILE`; the Rust built a fresh `Scanner` per call, so a second call to the `main` export restarted/lost buffered input. Made it a process-global scanner: 250 tokens now yield 100/200/250 lines for 1/2/3 calls in both.\n\n## Phases B–D\n\n66 harness tests + one `harness = false` binary (fd-1 capture is process-global and races with libtest, so in-process `driver` diffs live there; `driver_fn.rs` runs the same rows out-of-process via an `examples/so_runner.rs` dlopen helper). Randomized with a fixed-seed SplitMix64; `main` is checked four ways per input (C program, Rust program, C `.so` `main`, Rust `.so` `main`).\n\nI mutation-tested the harness three times (off-by-one in `fma_array`, wrong `strtol` clamp, dropped `\\v` from the whitespace set) — each was caught by the rows designed for it, then reverted.\n\n`./run_all.sh` runs everything green for all 4 artifact configurations: debug and release Rust, against both the default `-O0` C and a `-O2` C. All 40 `CONFIGS.md` rows and all 24 `ERRORS.md` rows pass; `c_src/` is untouched apart from the prescribed `build/` directory."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1029,"cache_read_input_tokens":174919,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1029,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"54b371d1-f01d-4012-be17-3d40a3cd679b","uuid":"dc536c10-00f6-44e1-b4ce-4812b31776bb","timestamp":"2026-08-18T08:02:02.106Z"}
